{"records":[{"id":"t2pmyzzt6n","title":"Ubiquitin receptor PSMD4/Rpn10 is a novel therapeutic target in multiple myeloma","description":"PSMD4/Rpn10 is a subunit of the 19S proteasome unit that is involved with feeding target proteins into the catalytic machinery of the proteasome. Given that inhibiting the proteasome is a common therapeutic strategy in multiple myeloma (MM), we investigated Rpn10 and found that it is highly expressed in MM cells, compared to PBMCs, and its expression is inversely correlated with overall survival and positively correlated with disease progression in MM patients. Inducible knockout or knockdown of Rpn10 decreased MM cell viability both in vitro and in vivo by triggering the accumulation of polyubiquitinated proteins, MM cell cycle arrest, and MM cell death through caspase-dependent- and unfolded-protein-response-related apoptosis. Proteomic analysis revealed that inhibiting Rpn10 in MM increased autophagy, antigen presentation and the activation of CD4+ T cells and NK cells. We then developed an in vitro AlphaScreen binding assay for high-throughput screening and identified a novel Rpn10 inhibitor, SB699551. Treating MM cell lines, leukemic cell lines, and primary MM patient cells with SB699551 significantly decreased cell viability without affecting the viability of normal PBMCs. SB699551 inhibited the proliferation of MM cells even in the presence of microenvironmental cells and without blocking the catalytic activity of the 20S proteasome unit or the deubiquitinating activity of the 19S unit. Rpn10 blockade by SB699551 triggered MM cell death via similar pathways as the genetic strategy. Xenograft animal models found that SB699551 was well-tolerated, inhibited tumor growth, and prolonged survival. Our data suggests that inhibiting Rpn10 will enhance cytotoxicity and overcome proteasome-inhibitor resistance in MM, providing the basis for further optimization studies of Rpn10 inhibitors for clinical application.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-12-06T17:30:06.304Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/t2pmyzzt6n","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/t2pmyzzt6n"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"ting","last_name":"du","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Multiple Myeloma"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"6vr8v59ykm","title":"Chikungunya virus infection disrupts MHC-I antigen presentation via nonstructural protein 2 ","description":"Arthritogenic alphaviruses, including chikungunya virus (CHIKV), are re-emerging global public health threats with no approved vaccines or antiviral therapies. Infection with these viruses causes debilitating musculoskeletal disease for months to years that is associated with the persistence of viral RNA and antigen. Prior studies using a mouse model found that CD8+ T cells, which recognize viral peptides in the context of major histocompatibility class I (MHC-I) displayed on the surface of infected cells, have a limited role in the control and clearance of CHIKV infection in joint-associated tissues, suggesting that CHIKV-infected cells evade these critical effectors of the antiviral immune response. Here, we show that MHC-I antigen presentation is inefficient in CHIKV-infected joint tissue fibroblasts, and that a protein encoded by CHIKV and produced in infected cells, nonstructural protein 2 (nsP2), disrupts the surface display of MHC-I molecules and antigen recognition of infected cells by CD8+ T cells. Our findings support a role for CHIKV nsP2 in the evasion of the CD8+ T cell response and establishment of persistent infection.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-05-12T19:51:36.848Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6vr8v59ykm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6vr8v59ykm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Tem","last_name":"Morrison","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Flow Cytometry","Antigen Presentation","Major Histocompatibility Complex","Cytotoxic T-Cell","Chikungunya Virus"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vdtjzmdgkk","title":"Ubiquitin receptor PSMD4/Rpn10 is a novel therapeutic target in multiple myeloma","description":"Abstract: PSMD4/Rpn10 is a subunit of the 19S proteasome unit that is involved with feeding target proteins into the catalytic machinery of the proteasome. Given that inhibiting the proteasome is a common therapeutic strategy in multiple myeloma (MM), we investigated Rpn10 and found that it is highly expressed in MM cells, compared to PBMCs, and its expression is inversely correlated with overall survival and positively correlated with disease progression in MM patients. Inducible knockout or knockdown of Rpn10 decreased MM cell viability both in vitro and in vivo by triggering the accumulation of polyubiquitinated proteins, MM cell cycle arrest, and MM cell death through caspase-dependent- and unfolded-protein-response-related apoptosis. Proteomic analysis revealed that inhibiting Rpn10 in MM increased autophagy, antigen presentation and the activation of CD4+ T cells and NK cells. We then developed an in vitro AlphaScreen binding assay for high-throughput screening and identified a novel Rpn10 inhibitor, SB699551. Treating MM cell lines, leukemic cell lines, and primary MM patient cells with SB699551 significantly decreased cell viability without affecting the viability of normal PBMCs. SB699551 inhibited the proliferation of MM cells even in the presence of microenvironmental cells and without blocking the catalytic activity of the 20S proteasome unit or the deubiquitinating activity of the 19S unit. Rpn10 blockade by SB699551 triggered MM cell death via similar pathways as the genetic strategy. Xenograft animal models found that SB699551 was well-tolerated, inhibited tumor growth, and prolonged survival. Our data suggests that inhibiting Rpn10 will enhance cytotoxicity and overcome proteasome-inhibitor resistance in MM, providing the basis for further optimization studies of Rpn10 inhibitors for clinical application.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-12-06T17:30:02.085Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vdtjzmdgkk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vdtjzmdgkk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"ting","last_name":"du","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Multiple Myeloma","Proteasome"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xjwbwrb35k","title":"The Receptor Tyrosine Kinase AXL is Required at Multiple Steps of the Metastatic Cascade during HER2-positive Breast Cancer Progression","description":"Western blots from The Receptor Tyrosine Kinase AXL is Required at Multiple Steps of the Metastatic Cascade during HER2-positive Breast Cancer Progression","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-03-19T15:51:16.129Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xjwbwrb35k","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xjwbwrb35k"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jean-Francois","last_name":"Côté","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"k392rcs98d","title":"The neuronal receptor tyrosine kinase Alk is a target for longevity","description":"Raw data to accompany the paper \"The neuronal receptor tyrosine kinase Alk is a target for longevity,\" Aging Cell (2020). https://doi.org/10.1111/acel.13137","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-03-08T09:26:19.206Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/k392rcs98d","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/k392rcs98d"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Nathan","last_name":"Woodling","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Animal Sleep","Western Blot","Real-Time Polymerase Chain Reaction","Insect Lifespan"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"fhyxg5h6pk","title":"The Multipass Translocon Modulates Ribosomal Frameshifting and Spike Protein Maturation in the Alphavirus Structural Polyprotein","description":"Like other enveloped RNA viruses, alphaviruses synthesize and assemble their envelope glycoproteins at the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) membrane. There, protein-conducting channels called translocons thread the nascent proteins across the lipid bilayer and help fold them into their correct shapes. We previously showed that a hydrophobic segment in the Sindbis virus structural polyprotein forms cotranslational interactions with the translocon that enhance −1 programmed ribosomal frameshifting (−1PRF), a recoding event that regulates polyprotein biogenesis. Recent discoveries suggest this segment, which corresponds to the second transmembrane domain of the E2 protein, could serve as a signal that recruits the multipass translocon (MPT). Here, we show that knocking out certain components of the MPT increases −1PRF efficiency. These shifts in recoding track with changes in the membrane topology of the nascent polyprotein and in its downstream proteolytic processing in a manner that ultimately reduces viral fitness. Together, our results indicate that −1PRF and spike protein maturation in alphaviruses is tuned by the dynamic remodeling of the ER translocon. Such coupling could allow polyprotein biogenesis to adapt to different stages of viral replication and to distinct host or vector environments. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-07-23T22:44:10.741Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fhyxg5h6pk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fhyxg5h6pk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Antonio","last_name":"Bonifasi","orcid":"0009-0002-2229-5404"},{"first_name":"Gayani Apsara","last_name":"Ranasinghe","orcid":"0009-0001-6166-0630"},{"first_name":"Ashish R. ","last_name":"Jhangiani","orcid":"0000-0002-9545-9775"},{"first_name":"Venkatesh P. ","last_name":"Thirumalaikumar","orcid":"0000-0002-5009-1460"},{"first_name":"Brianna","last_name":"Corman","orcid":null},{"first_name":"J. Paul ","last_name":"Robinson","orcid":"0000-0001-8383-3240"},{"first_name":"Suchetana","last_name":"Mukhopadhyay","orcid":"0000-0001-9230-4746"},{"first_name":"Jonathan","last_name":"Schlebach","orcid":"0000-0003-0955-7633"}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/04q48ey07","name":"National Institute of General Medical Sciences","grant_id":"R35152086"}],"categories":["Virology","Flow Cytometry","Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hs467zj2cw","title":"Human genetic variation reveals FCRL3 is a lymphocyte receptor for Yersinia pestis. Keener et al.","description":"These data accompany the manuscript \"Human genetic variation reveals FCRL3 is a lymphocyte receptor for Yersinia pestis.\" Yersinia pestis is the bacterium responsible for plague, one of the deadliest diseases in history. To discover human genetic determinants of Y. pestis infection, we utilized nearly a thousand genetically diverse lymphoblastoid cell lines in a cellular genome-wide association study. A nonsynonymous SNP, rs2282284 (N721S), in Fc receptor like 3 (FCRL3) was associated with bacterial invasion of host cells (p=9x10-8). Overexpressed FCRL3 facilitated attachment and invasion of Y. pestis and colocalized with Y. pestis at attachment sites. These properties were variably conserved across the FCRL family, revealing an Ig-like domain and signaling motifs shared by FCRL3 and FCRL5 to be necessary for attachment and invasion. Direct binding to FCRL5 extracellular domain was confirmed, and B cells (the primary cells that express FCRLs) were preferentially invaded by Y. pestis. Thus, Y. pestis hijacks FCRL proteins, possibly taking advantage of an immune receptor to create a lymphocyte niche during infection. \nSummary statistics for the Hi-HOST GWAS of Y. pestis invasion were previously deposited at the Duke Data Repository (https://doi.org/10.7924/r43n2d008).","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-05-07T22:57:25.019Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hs467zj2cw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hs467zj2cw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Dennis","last_name":"Ko","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rachel","last_name":"Keener","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Human Genetics","Yersinia","Host-Pathogen Interaction"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"59dgnyx94z","title":" Trem2 prevents adverse pregnancy outcomes induced by Toxoplasma gondii by promoting PPARγ-mediated P-STAT6 signaling","description":"Decidual macrophages (DMs), required for the maintenance of a successful pregnancy, are vital target cells for Toxoplasma gondii (T. gondii) during adverse pregnancy induced by T. gondii. Triggering receptor expressed on myeloid cells 2 (Trem2) is a functional immune receptor on the surface of DMs, governing cell survival and phagocytosis.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-10T14:11:27.888Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/59dgnyx94z","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/59dgnyx94z"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"cao","last_name":"yining","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Parasite Immunology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"x4483f3rmk","title":"Dataset for 'LRP8 is an entry receptor for tick-borne encephalitis viruses'","description":"This dataset contains the raw data supporting all main and supplementary figures used in the manuscript \"LRP8 is an entry receptor for tick-borne encephalitis viruses\" published in PNAS. In this study, we expressed 13 different family members on the surface of cells and demonstrated that LRP8 promotes infection of tick-borne encephalitis viruses (TBEV). Validation studies confirmed direct binding of TBEV and LRP8, and a key role for LRP8 in neuronal cell infection. Our findings also enabled the generation of an inhibitory LRP8-Fc decoy molecule that blocks TBEV infection. ","source":{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-03-16T19:59:15.375Z","url":"https://digitalcommonsdata.wustl.edu/datasets/x4483f3rmk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/x4483f3rmk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Pengfei","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sean","last_name":"Hui","orcid":"0000-0002-9870-6823"},{"first_name":"Zhenlu","last_name":"Chong","orcid":"0000-0001-6195-3452"},{"first_name":"Olivier","last_name":"Escaffre","orcid":"0000-0003-0402-9698"},{"first_name":"Michael N.","last_name":"Nguyen","orcid":"0000-0002-4184-7078"},{"first_name":"Stefanie P.","last_name":"Muraro","orcid":"0000-0002-5105-6659"},{"first_name":"Hana","last_name":"Janova","orcid":"0000-0003-2578-0982"},{"first_name":"Hongming","last_name":"Ma","orcid":"0000-0001-5105-1332"},{"first_name":"Shiqi","last_name":"Cao","orcid":"0009-0006-9371-1216"},{"first_name":"Tomasz","last_name":"Kaszuba","orcid":"0000-0002-5967-4670"},{"first_name":"Brian","last_name":"Imbiakha","orcid":"0000-0001-8538-5445"},{"first_name":"Sathvik","last_name":"Palakurty","orcid":"0000-0002-2222-4553"},{"first_name":"Madison L.","last_name":"Pearson","orcid":"0009-0005-9931-9269"},{"first_name":"David A.","last_name":"Price","orcid":"0000-0002-0751-9943"},{"first_name":"Gaya K.","last_name":"Amarasinghe","orcid":"0000-0002-0418-9707"},{"first_name":"Daisy W.","last_name":"Leung","orcid":"0000-0002-7189-9557"},{"first_name":"Shannan L.","last_name":"Rossi","orcid":"0000-0003-2450-2374"},{"first_name":"Alexander N.","last_name":"Freiberg","orcid":"0000-0001-8413-8960"},{"first_name":"Daved H.","last_name":"Fremont","orcid":"0000-0002-8544-2689"},{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Diamond","orcid":"0000-0002-8791-3165"}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/043z4tv69","name":"National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases","grant_id":"U01AI073755"},{"id":"https://ror.org/043z4tv69","name":"National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases","grant_id":"U19AI181960"}],"categories":["Microbiology","Virology"],"journal_issn":["10916490"]},{"id":"3thfbs4y72","title":"The vitamin D receptor is essential for the replication of pseudorabies virus","description":"Dear ASM Journals Ethics, \nFirstly, we would like to thank you for your valuable contribution to the seamless publication of our article. Secondly, we apologize for the issues with the original data that we submitted. We have addressed your questions individually. Please review and let us know if there are any further concerns. We will do our utmost to assist you in completing the review process, ensuring that the article progresses smoothly to the next stage.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-09-19T17:47:43.732Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3thfbs4y72","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3thfbs4y72"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"shengli","last_name":"ming","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Original Order"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":122152,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":95,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":122152,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":121683,"restricted":58,"embargoed":411},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":1909},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":606},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":430},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":387},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoame